185	Q0	zis1	0	3.128891	instruments_tools	The instrument covers individual aspects of working life and the general attitude to work. The 37 items represent four dimensions self realization resignation satisfaction with payment evaluation of the company.
185	Q0	sg-38	1	2.1345763	instruments_tools	Mixing survey modes for data collection can have positive effects on response rates sample balance and survey costs. However data collected in multiple modes may also suffer from mode measurement effects. In this Survey Guideline we give an overview of empirical evidence related to the benefits and drawbacks of using multiple modes for data collection and outline some recommendations for the implementation of mixed mode surveys. Finally we provide a brief outlook on the perspectives of mixed mode surveys in the survey landscape.
185	Q0	zis92	2	2.0949445	instruments_tools	The instrument records whether life changing events have occurred in the last two years and how they have been subjectively experienced. Life changing events are relevant because they require readjustment and can contribute to the outbreak of a disease.
185	Q0	zis147	3	2.07639	instruments_tools	This five item scale is used to measure life satisfaction. According to Diener 1984 this is a multifactorial construct with affective and cognitive evaluative components. The affective components are characterized by the presence of positive and the absence of negative emotions. The cognitive evaluative components consist of global and domain specific satisfaction in various areas of life. This five item scale was developed by Diener Emmons Larsen and Griffin 1985 and translated into German by Schumacher 2003 . It serves to record life satisfaction.
185	Q0	zis354	4	2.0484834	instruments_tools	Main status and employment are two complementary socio economic characteristics of a person. The main status describes social groups based on their current subjective main activity or status e.g. pupils self employed unemployed or retirees . It also describes an individual s position relative to the labour market which is closely linked to the life cycle. The main status strongly influences a person s everyday life behaviour experience and social relationships and is often used to select relevant subgroups for analyses. Gainful employment is conceptualised separately as it can not only be the main status as is usually the case with full time employees for example but can also be exercised part time alongside another main status e.g. students who work part time . In the case of output harmonisation of these characteristics only mediocre comparability can be expected as the measurement instrument in the questionnaire has a major influence on the response behaviour of respondents. It is therefore preferable to standardise the questionnaire items themselves.
185	Q0	zis42	5	2.006002	instruments_tools	This questionnaire captures the importance that people attach to central areas of life.
185	Q0	zis207	6	1.9450319	instruments_tools	Quality of life research deals with the life contents of people and the satisfaction associated with them. This scale records the identification with the residential area and as a consequence the satisfaction of the interviewees with living conditions according to certain specified criteria e.g. child friendliness air quality traffic safety . If the assessment is negative this results in dissatisfaction. If it is positive more satisfaction with the living situation is experienced.
185	Q0	zis239	7	1.8223388	instruments_tools	The Balanced Short Scale of Authoritarian Attitudes B RWA 6 presented below was developed and used as part of the Austrian National Election Study AUTNES for general population samples electorate . The aim of the scale was a broad but economic recording of right wing authoritarianism RWA cf. Altemeyer 1981 as well as the application of semantically oppositely polarized items in their measurement. The attempt was made to cover three central facets or subdimensions of the superordinate construct A. the demand for the acceptance of supposed authorities and the norms represented by them as belonging to authority B. support for the punishment of individuals deviating from the norm as authoritarian aggression and C. the demand for adherence to valid norms or traditional traditions as authoritarian conventionalism . Detailed information on the theoretical embedding and quality of the scale can be found in the following sections.
185	Q0	zis284	8	1.8197486	instruments_tools	The General Life Satisfaction Short Scale L 1 described herein measures general life satisfaction with one item completion time 10 s . The scale is the English language adaptation of the German language scale Kurzskala zur Erfassung der Allgemeinen Lebenszufriedenheit. The item of the German language source version was translated into English using the TRAPD approach. Our empirical validation based on a heterogeneous quota sample in the UK showed that the reliability and validity coefficients of the English language adaptation are satisfactory and comparable to those of the German language source version. As an ultra short scale L 1 lends itself to the assessment of general life satisfaction particularly in survey contexts in which assessment time or questionnaire space are limited. The scale is applicable in a variety of research disciplines including psychology sociology and economics.
185	Q0	zis229	9	1.8058654	instruments_tools	The short scale L 1 for recording general life satisfaction consists of the item formulation established in SOEP Richter Metzing Weinhardt Schupp 2013 . The scale contains only one item with the following wording How satisfied are you at present all in all with your life . The 11 answer categories of the L 1 range from not satisfied at all to completely satisfied . The name of the answer categories has been modified and thus differs from that of the SOEP.
185	Q0	zis232	10	1.7832774	instruments_tools	The scale captures different views on the meaning of life theism humanism and nihilism and was used in the General Population Survey of the Social Sciences ALLBUS 2012 among others.
185	Q0	zis26	11	1.7308657	instruments_tools	The scale shows the quality of the relationship between employee and superior. The aspects of trust respect encouragement and affection are taken into account. The questionnaire contains item formulations that are as close to everyday life as possible.
185	Q0	zis199	12	1.7140732	instruments_tools	The scale measures youth activities in the social spaces of streets squares and parks. It consists of the dimensions occupation Deviante activities coping with everyday life coping with the future opportunities for contact and opportunities for development .
185	Q0	zis35	13	1.6050682	instruments_tools	The ASKU is an economic instrument for recording individual competence expectations to be able to deal with difficulties and obstacles in daily life. The reported evidence on the quality of the ASKU suggests that the scale allows a reliable valid and economic assessment of subjective competence expectations in social science research.
185	Q0	zis81	14	1.6050682	instruments_tools	The questionnaire corresponds to the German version of the Right Wing authoritarianism scale by Altemeyer. Altmeyer assumes three sub dimensions of authoritarian personality Authoritarian subservience authoritarian aggression conventionalism. According to Altemeyer authoritarianism is influenced by the social environment in different phases of life.
185	Q0	zis88	15	1.5906178	instruments_tools	The biographical questionnaire for alcohol addicts maps the personality dimensions 1 Emotional Insufficiency or Neuroticism 2 Unfavourable Favourable Primary Socialisation and 3 Aiming. The biographical inventory constructed here differs from pure personality questionnaires by a specific focus on life history.
185	Q0	dbd-14	16	1.5566823	instruments_tools	Digital behavioral data DBD has become an invaluable resource for researchers across the social sciences offering unprecedented insights into human behavior and social phenomena. The collection and use of such data raise significant ethical concerns that researchers must carefully navigate. This guide provides an overview of the ethical considerations in DBD research addressing both platform centered and user centered approaches. It explores key ethical challenges throughout the research process from study design and data collection to processing and analysis publication and data sharing. It discusses critical issues such as informed consent data minimization privacy or inferring user attributes. In addition to outlining key ethical issues the guide discusses helpful resources and provides recommendations to help researchers conduct ethically sound DBD studies while maximizing the value of their research. As the landscape of DBD research continues to evolve this guide is meant to serve as a resource for researchers ethics committees and policymakers seeking to balance scientific advancement with the protection of individual rights and privacy in research with DBD.
185	Q0	zis78	17	1.5220997	instruments_tools	Mental impairment and psychosomatic complaints correspond to the cognitive emotional experience of a reduced quality of life as a long term consequence of everyday and ongoing stressors. The theoretical basis for the scale documented here is the transactional cognitive stress model of Lazarus 1966 . The scale was developed for use in occupational health psychological research and practice.
185	Q0	zis308_exz	18	1.504578	instruments_tools	The ICT SC25 is a 25 item scale to economically processing time 1 min per scale assess com petence self concept SC related to the use of information and communication technology ICT available in the two languages German ICT SC25g and English ICT SC25e . The ICT SC25g e measures general and domain specific ICT SC communicate process and store generate content safe application and solve problems in the adult population 18 69 years across contexts private life work and education . The present study documents the development and validation of the ICT SC25g e. The results indicate good reliability internal consistency test retest reliability . Confirma tory factor analyses support factorial validity and theory consistent relations with personality traits e.g. emotional stability and technology related measures e.g. frequency of ICT use technopho bia confirm construct validity. The scale shows strict measurement invariance across gender and countries.
185	Q0	zis8	19	1.4337856	instruments_tools	The scale alienation from work records with 10 items whether persons ascribe a meaning to their work or regard it as actually meaningless.
185	Q0	zis243	20	1.4321963	instruments_tools	The four item scale measures work family conflict as a two directional process work interference with family and family interference with work. The items have been used since 2002 in the Family and Changing Gender Roles module of the International Social Survey Programme ISSP .
185	Q0	zis7	21	1.3980899	instruments_tools	The scale Work as a means of securing one s livelihood contains 9 items that show the extent to which work is viewed under the aspect of money acquisition.
185	Q0	zis259	22	1.2262863	instruments_tools	The temporal focus describes the amount of attention people pay to the temporal categories past present and future. In contrast to the related construct of the time perspective the authors of the Temporal Focus Scale TFS assume that different temporal focuses are independent dimensions. This documentation contains the translation and adaptation process of the English language TFS into German and presents initial results on its reliability and validity. In addition to the three subscales past present and own future a scale relevant to sustainability research distant future is developed and tested which focuses on future periods beyond one s own life.
